Branch Tournament Analysis
  vertices: representative n=18 branch rows
  observable: has-gate, unit witness loss, gap, endpoint debt, descendant denominator
  switch: more counterexample-shaped row points to less; tie path is listed row order
  H=1 c3=0 SCCs=(1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1) score_hist=0:1,1:1,2:1,3:1,4:1,5:1,6:1,7:1,8:1
  top branch rows: double-gate 36* skip 8:8 | gate ladder 18* skip 8:7 | lpd ladder 9* skip 8:6 | swap 8->108:5 | initial replace 17 by 18:4 | initial replace 8 by 18:3

Interpretation
  THM-382/383 say the right LRC quotient must remember threshold colors and boundary tie-wall compactification.
  HYP-1981 turns that decorated target into source reachability: every lonely witness listed above is an observer-source marked tournament.
  The n=18 unit skeleton behaves like the old n=14 gate/tightness skeleton: without an 18-multiple, all six unit points remain safe.
  Therefore the no-18-gate branch cannot be an open-cover counterexample. It can only be tight/boundary-like or positive-gap with unit witnesses intact.
  The has-18-gate branch kills the unit witnesses but exports debt to descendant endpoint denominators.  In HYP-1981 language, it kills the unit source targets but moves source reachability into descendant layers.
  The next proof object should be an observer-source endpoint-debt certificate for the 18-gate/lpd branch, split into 2-torsion and 3^2-torsion descendant layers.
